Insert walks the tree one character at a time, creating a child the moment one doesn't exist yet, then marks the final node as a word end.

```python
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.is_end = False

def insert(root, word):
    node = root
    for ch in word:
        if ch not in node.children:
            node.children[ch] = TrieNode()
        node = node.children[ch]
    node.is_end = True
```

This is O(L) where L is the word's length — never O(dictionary size). Inserting `"car"` after `"cart"` reuses the existing `c-a-r` path and just flips `is_end` on the node at `r`.

Both operations are the same walk down the tree — they differ only in what they check when the walk finishes.

```python
def _find_node(root, s):
    node = root
    for ch in s:
        if ch not in node.children:
            return None
        node = node.children[ch]
    return node

def search(root, word):
    node = _find_node(root, word)
    return node is not None and node.is_end

def starts_with(root, prefix):
    return _find_node(root, prefix) is not None
```

`search` requires the walk to end at a node marked `is_end`; `starts_with` only requires the walk to complete. Confusing the two is the single most common trie bug in interviews.
